relation:between, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/a644e277.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/a644e277.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nExtract the lattice subregion bounded by the four background-colored corner holes.\n\nInput structure:\n- A regular one-cell-thick lattice has one line color, one background color,\n  and sparse marker pixels.\n- Exactly four lattice-line intersections are background-colored holes instead\n  of line-color pixels.\n\nRule:\n- Find the unique non-background color that forms regular horizontal and\n  vertical lattice lines.\n- Use the four background-colored lattice intersections as the corners of one\n  rectangle.\n- Return the inclusive crop between those corner rows and columns.\n\nKey insight:\n- The crop is marked by the missing lattice intersections, not by the marker\n  pixels inside the cells.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"symmetry/pattern\",\n  \"src\": \"regular one-cell-thick lattice with exactly four background-colored holes at lattice intersections\",\n  \"transform\": \"output the inclusive rectangular crop whose four corners are those holes; preserve all pixels inside the region\",\n  \"grid_shape\": \"crop to the hole-corner rectangle\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"2\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"9f669b64\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3571\nmechanism=0.3807\nlexical=0.0111\ncode_feature=0.8000\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:connected_components, code:output_resize, code:rotation, object:axis, object:grid_edge, object:rectangle, object:sprite, relation:between, relation:direction, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/9f669b64.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/9f669b64.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nPush the central solid rectangle to the grid edge, splitting the perpendicular target rectangle around its path.\n\nInput structure\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- There are exactly three monochrome foreground objects arranged on one\n  horizontal or vertical axis.\n- The middle object on that axis is a solid rectangular moving object.\n- One end object is a solid rectangle or bar whose long side is perpendicular\n  to the movement axis; this is the target to split.\n\nRule\n- Move the middle rectangle along the axis toward the target until it is\n  flush with the grid edge in that direction.\n- Clear the original middle rectangle and the original target.\n- Split the target through the moving rectangle's two-cell-wide path and\n  shift the two halves one cell outward perpendicular to the movement.\n- Keep the other end object unchanged.\n\nKey insight\n- The non-target end object acts as a pusher; the central rectangle travels\n  away from it through the perpendicular target, opening a matching gap.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"move_along_path\",\n  \"src\": \"middle solid rectangular object between two end objects\",\n  \"path\": \"straight horizontal or vertical line away from the non-target end object and through the perpendicular solid target rectangle/bar\",\n  \"dst\": \"place flush with the grid edge beyond the target\",\n  \"trajectory\": \"paint only the final moved rectangle at dst\",\n  \"transform\": \"erase original source pixels; split the target rectangle/bar along the moving object's two-cell-wide path and shift its two halves one cell outward perpendicular to the movement; keep the other end object unchanged\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"3\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"3cd86f4f\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3743\nmechanism=0.4404\nlexical=0.0142\ncode_feature=0.6000\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:output_resize, code:rotation, object:grid_edge, object:rectangle, object:sprite, operation:reflect, relation:adjacent, relation:direction, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/3cd86f4f.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/3cd86f4f.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nShear the rectangular input down-left onto a black canvas.\n\nInput structure\nA rectangular grid of colored pixels with no internal empty region to infer.\n\nRule\nCreate a black output with the same height and width W + H - 1, then copy\neach input row r unchanged starting at column H - 1 - r.\n\nKey insight\nThe colors and row contents are preserved; only the row offsets change so\nthe left edge forms a descending diagonal.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"symmetry/pattern\",\n  \"src\": \"the full input rectangle, interpreted as rows to be diagonally staggered\",\n  \"dst\": \"black canvas with the same height and enough columns for the staggered rows\",\n  \"transform\": \"copy every row unchanged; place row r starting at column H - 1 - r so adjacent rows shift one column left downward\",\n  \"grid_shape\": \"same height, width W + H - 1\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"4\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"faa9f03d\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3560\nmechanism=0.4310\nlexical=0.0144\ncode_feature=0.5000\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:connected_components, code:output_resize, object:axis, object:grid_edge, object:marker, object:rectangle, object:sprite, operation:reflect, relation:between, relation:direction, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/faa9f03d.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/faa9f03d.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nRepair a one-cell-thick colored line network by replacing singleton markers, filling short orthogonal gaps, and redirecting yellow-marked arms to the edge.\n\nInput structure:\n- A rectangular grid with a dominant background.\n- Line colors form one-cell-thick horizontal/vertical stroke fragments.\n- Marker colors are exactly those whose 4-connected components are all singleton pixels.\n- Red singleton markers are missing line cells; yellow singleton markers are directional controls next to a line endpoint.\n\nRule:\n- Assign every marker to the nearest visible line color in row/column line-of-sight, preferring an endpoint supported along that same axis.\n- For each line color, fill the gap between consecutive facing endpoints in a row or column.\n- A yellow control erases the old straight arm on the far side of its endpoint and paints the same color from that endpoint through the control pixel to the grid edge.\n- At crossings, yellow-control rays win; otherwise an original-vs-filled crossing uses the more fragmented pre-repair line color, while pure filled crossings use the lower color id.\n\nKey insight: The singleton markers identify which sparse stroke fragments belong together; after they are colored, the intended lines are the axis-aligned gaps between facing endpoints, with yellow controls flipping one arm to the marked side.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"transform_in_place\",\n  \"src\": \"one-cell-thick colored line network with singleton red/yellow marker pixels\",\n  \"transform\": \"replace each marker by its assigned line color, fill facing horizontal/vertical gaps, and use yellow controls to erase the old arm and paint the same color from the endpoint through the marker to the grid edge\",\n  \"notes\": \"crossings prefer yellow-control rays; otherwise original-vs-filled crossings use the more fragmented repaired line, and marker-bracketed filled gaps beat unmarked pure fills\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"5\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"458e3a53\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3634\nmechanism=0.4251\nlexical=0.0116\ncode_feature=0.6000\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:output_resize, code:rotation, object:axis, object:rectangle, object:sprite, operation:crop_resize, operation:reflect, output:crop, relation:direction, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/458e3a53.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/458e3a53.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nExtract the compact table of solid cells hidden inside a separator lattice.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid has full-width and full-height bars of one separator color, dividing it into rectangular cells.\n- Most cells contain a repeating multicolor pattern, while a contiguous rectangle of cells is solid monochrome.\n\nRule\n- Split the input into lattice cells using the full separator rows and columns.\n- Keep the cells whose full interior is one color, crop to their occupied rectangle, and output those colors.\n\nKey insight\n- The periodic pattern is noise; only completely solid lattice cells encode the reduced output grid.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"symmetry/pattern\",\n  \"src\": \"solid monochrome cells inside a full-row/full-column separator lattice over a periodic pattern\",\n  \"dst\": \"compact rectangle of solid-cell colors, preserving lattice row and column order\",\n  \"transform\": \"split at separator bars; identify cells whose interior is a single color; crop to the occupied solid-cell rectangle and replace each cell by its color\",\n  \"grid_shape\": \"height and width equal to the occupied solid-cell rows and columns\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"6\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"b10624e5\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3242\nmechanism=0.3318\nlexical=0.0128\ncode_feature=0.8000\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:connected_components, code:output_resize, code:rotation, object:axis, object:quadrant, operation:reflect, relation:adjacent, relation:direction, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/b10624e5.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/b10624e5.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nReplicate a motif across quadrants with scaling and reflection keyed to red anchor blocks.\n\nInput structure:\n- Grid divided into four quadrants by a full-row and full-column divider of a single non-bg color.\n- Each quadrant contains a red (2) square anchor block of varying size.\n- The top-left quadrant also has a colored motif attached to its red anchor.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
